# New codex pipeline

Follow these steps in order. Stop at each gate until the user says to continue. Use `Tyranids 300/` as the layout reference (not the rulebook). Copy Tyranid *layout*, not Tyranid rules or section list.

French authors are now writing **v3.1.0** codices (folder digits **310**). The copies in `Originals/` are early versions, not finished French books. Treat them as drafts: expect later French updates, and do not treat a 3.1.0 PDF as final. Finished English books for v3.0.0 stay in `300` folders; a 3.1.0 translation gets its own `310` folder.

## A — Audit the French original

Read the French PDF in `Originals/` page by page. Report inconsistencies (see `audit-french-before-translate`). Cover at least summary tables vs unit cards, army lists vs profiles, named abilities vs special-rules text, and French terms/numbers written two ways. Do not treat rulebook abilities that a codex only names (without reprinting the rule) as inconsistencies; print the name only.

Different costs for the same unit in different formations are not errors. Name and weapon spelling clashes use the official Games Workshop English name. If an ability might sit on the unit or the weapon, put it on the weapon. Fix grammar; do not ask. Example armies are often wrong: if that is the only mismatch, follow the real army list and profiles.

Numbered IDs (`1`, `2`, `3`…) need a user choice. Auto-resolved issues still appear, **at the end**, as `1A`, `2A`… with `Chosen` filled.

Table columns:

| ID | Topic | Description | Suggestion A | Suggestion B | Note |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `1`, `2`, `3`… then `1A`, `2A`… | Short label (`Price Shield Drones`) | What the problem is | First source to follow | Second source to follow | Extra suggestions or context; often blank |

The user replies with the issue number and the solution letter for numbered IDs only (for example `1 A, 2 B`).

Gate: show the table and wait. Do not start B until the user has seen it and chosen which source to follow for each numbered ID.

## B — Create the book folder

Folder name: English faction name + French version digits with no dots (Tyranids v3.0.0 → `Tyranids 300`; v3.1.0 → `Tyranids 310`). Local `preamble.tex` is `\input{../shared/codex-preamble}`. Add `main.tex` in the Tyranids pattern (A4, title, author, date). Add `.tex` files as sections appear in C. Kebab-case file names; no TOC.

## C — Translate (no unit images)

Walk the French PDF page by page (or one unit/section at a time if that is more accurate). Skip the French cover, table of contents, army examples, and marker/template pages. Keep legal notice and introduction if present. Match Tyranids heading levels, tables, `\unitdivider`, and GW English names. Split landscape unit-profile tables so each page stays inside A4 (see the unit-profile-tables rule). Do not add `\unitimage` or extract portraits.

Gate: tell the user the book is translated. Remind them to rebuild the PDF. They inspect PDFs, not `.tex`. Wait.

## D — User inspection

Do not start E until the user has inspected the rebuilt PDF and said to continue.

## E — Compare original to `.tex`

Inspect `.tex` files, not compiled English PDFs. Run two separate rounds and report each:

1. Headings: `\section` / `\subsection` / `\subsubsection` match the French original and the Tyranids pattern.
2. Values: every unit stat, weapon, ability, cost, and army-list line matches the chosen source from A.

Gate: wait for the user before F. If they will inspect layout, remind them to rebuild the PDF.

## F — Unit images

Extract portraits from the French PDF. Crop inside the picture frame so no black border, page texture, or wrapping text remains. Place `\unitimage{name.png}` after each unit table, same as Tyranids.
